2. Some Results 
2.1. The Range of degree 
Theorem 1. In a Ramsey graph of r(p,q) with n vertices,the degree of every vertex is in 
the range of [n − r(p,q − 1),r(p − 1,q) − 1][27]. 
Proof. For any vertex u,we suppose the degree of u is d. Then there are d vertices 
adjacent to u. 
• Thus d < r (p − 1,q).If not u, then this d vertex will contain a p − 1 55 complete 
graph. With the addition of u,there will be a p complete graph. 
• Thus n − 1 − d < r(p,q − 1). If not u, and n − 1 − d vertices which are not adjacent 
to u will form a q dependent set. 

=== PAGE 5 ===
5 
So n − 1 − r(p,q − 1) < d < r (p − 1,q). Since all of degrees are integer so d is in 
the range of [n − r(p,q − 1),r(p − 1,q) − 1]. 
